course: dictionary
lesson: 4596
title: Here
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Here» is a small word that points to a place, a moment or a case. It most often means in this place, at this spot, or to this spot. «Come here» means come to the spot where I am, and «over here» points to a place near the speaker.
explain: «Here» can also mean at this point or in this case. In «Here the word more does not mean adding», the word here tells you that in this problem, more has a different job. Read the words around here, and they will tell you which meaning to use.
explain: When you give or show something, say «here you are» or «here it is». «I'm here if you want to talk» means I am present and ready to help. The same word, here, can mean where, when or how, so always check the sentence around it.
fact: Here is an adverb: it tells you where, or in what case, something happens.
fact: «Here» means in, at or to this place, as in «come here» or «put it here».
fact: «Here» can mean in this case or at this point, as in «here we multiply».
fact: «Here is» and «here you are» are said when you give or show something to someone.
fact: In class, «Here, Miss!» means I am present, and it answers the register.
exercise: «Come here, and show me your drawing.» What does here mean? | Long ago, in the past | *In this place, near me | At the shop down the road | Over there, far away
exercise: In maths: «Here the word more does not mean adding.» What does here mean? | In this place, near the board | Once upon a time | *In this case, at this point | Here you are, take it
exercise: Ravi gives his friend a pencil and says, «Here you are.» What is he doing? | Counting the pupils | Asking where his friend is | *Giving or showing something | Leaving the room
test: Which sentence uses here to mean at this point? | Come here and sit by me. | Put the bag over there, by the door. | Here you are, a sweet for you. | *Here we stop and check our answers.
test: Tia says, «I'm here if you want to talk.» What does here mean? | *Present and ready to help | Giving something to someone | In this place, by the window | Answering the register in class
exam: Which meaning fits «Here, Miss!» when the register is called? | At this point in the story | Here is a book for you | *Here, I am present in class | In this place, near the door
